              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This statement is true, due to the facts that the drug spends very little time in the stomach and there is comparatively little surface area for absorption in the stomach.</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>This statement is true, due to the facts that the drug spends very little time in the stomach and there is comparatively little surface area for absorption in the stomach.</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Even if the properties of the drug favour absorption in the stomach, as they do with aspirin, very little of that medication is actually absorbed in the stomach.&nbsp;&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, it is the lipophilic (or lipid soluble) drugs that are the ones that are absorbed the fastest in the GIT. Water soluble drugs (hydrophilic drugs) need to find and use channels in the GIT, while lipid soluble drugs (lipophilic drugs) can be absorbed through the cell membranes.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Indeed, it is the lipophilic (or lipid soluble) drugs that are the ones that are absorbed the fastest in the GIT. Water soluble drugs (hydrophilic drugs) need to find and use channels in the GIT, while lipid soluble drugs (lipophilic drugs) can be absorbed through the cell membranes.</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Hydrophilic (water soluble) drugs are the type of drugs that are absorbed the best from the GIT\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>When a drug is taken with food, it normally decreases the bioavailability (the fraction of the drug that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged) of the drug</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Yes, an ointment is oil based, normally about 80% oil. Creams have about equal parts oil to water based components.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>You should have selected this choice as being correct. An ointment is oil based, normally about 80% oil. Creams have about equal parts oil to water based components.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>An ointment is an oil based topical preparation. If it includes a medication that must be absorbed, that medication needs to be a lipophilic drug&nbsp;</div>\n"
                }
              ],
